---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import WaitlistCta from '../components/WaitlistCta.astro';

const values = [
  { title: 'Protect the most targeted', body: 'Image-based abuse falls hardest on kids, teens, and women. We build for them first.' },
  { title: 'Privacy is non-negotiable', body: 'A safety tool that harvests your data is not a safety tool. Everything runs on your device, with nothing to upload and nothing to leak.' },
  { title: 'Honesty over hype', body: 'We tell you what works, what doesn’t yet, and where the edges are. Confidence you can verify beats claims you have to believe.' },
];
---

<Layout
  title="About SafeScreen"
  description="SafeScreen began with people we know being harmed by unwanted explicit content and online abuse. Our mission: put the safety check on the device, in real time, for the people most targeted."
>
  <section class="container-tight pt-20 pb-4 sm:pt-28">
    <p class="eyebrow">Our mission</p>
    <h1 class="mt-5 max-w-3xl text-[2.4rem] font-bold leading-[1.06] tracking-[-0.03em] sm:text-5xl">
      A safety layer for the people most targeted.
    </h1>
  </section>

  <!-- story -->
  <section class="container-tight mt-12">
    <div class="max-w-2xl space-y-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-fg-2">
      <p>
        A friend's younger sister was harassed with explicit images online. A kid we know stumbled onto
        content no kid should see. Each time, the pattern repeated: by the time anyone could react, the
        harmful content had already been seen, and you can't un-see it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The tools meant to stop this all lived in the cloud. To ask "is this image safe?", a victim first
        has to upload their most private moment to someone else's server. The safeguard was its own kind of
        exposure.
      </p>
      <p class="text-fg">So we made a different bet: put the safety check at the edge. On the device, in real time, private by construction.</p>
      <p>
        SafeScreen started as a focused engineering build. We proved a compact AI model could run fast
        enough, on a phone, to blur harmful content before you see it, without your screen ever leaving
        the device. It worked. Now we're turning it into something everyone can put on their phone.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- stat -->
  <section class="container-tight mt-24">
    <div class="border-t border-line-soft pt-10">
      <div class="mono text-5xl text-fg sm:text-6xl">58%</div>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-xl text-lg text-fg-2">
        of teens who have seen explicit content online stumbled onto it by accident. Exposure isn't a
        choice anymore. The defense has to live where the harm lands: on the screen, in the moment.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- values -->
  <section class="container-tight mt-32">
    <p class="eyebrow">What we stand for</p>
    <h2 class="mt-5 text-3xl sm:text-4xl">Three commitments we won't trade away.</h2>
    <div class="mt-10 grid gap-px overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-line bg-line md:grid-cols-3">
      {
        values.map((v, i) => (
          <div class="bg-surface p-7">
            <div class="mono text-sm text-accent">0{i + 1}</div>
            <h3 class="mt-4 text-xl">{v.title}</h3>
            <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-fg-2">{v.body}</p>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- vision -->
  <section class="container-tight mt-24">
    <div class="max-w-2xl">
      <p class="eyebrow">Where this goes</p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-2xl leading-snug text-fg sm:text-3xl">
        A private, real-time safety layer for everyone, starting with the people who need it most.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-fg-3">Next on the roadmap: faster on-device acceleration, region-precise blur, and text-based protection.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CTA -->
  <WaitlistCta />
</Layout>
